issues.body:
Instead of hard-coding the Backstabbing enchantment's sensitivity and damage, how easy would it be to implement a conditional instead and use JSON enchantment definition for the rest of it? For example:
```
{
  . . .
  "effects": {
    "minecraft:damage": [
      {
        "effect": {
          "type": "minecraft:multiply",
          "factor": {
            "type": "minecraft:linear",
            "base": 1.2,
            "per_level_above_first": 0.2
          }
        },
        "requirements": {
          "condition": "farmersdelight:entity_rotation",
          "entity": "this",
          "offset": 90
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  . . .
}
```
(i.e. `entity_rotation` is true if the target entity is rotated 90 or more degrees away from the attacker)
It'd be a useful conditional anyways, but it's also more forward-facing in terms of components and avoiding hard-coding, and allows configuration of the enchantment through datapacks.

```
// Use the LivingEntity sensitive version in favour of this.
   @Deprecated
   public void eat(Item pItem, ItemStack pStack) {
      this.eat(pItem, pStack, null);
   }
```

This method passes null as the LivingEntity paramether. My mod's mixin requires that LivingEntity is not null and it is not fixable from my side (actually is with null check, but then I'll lose core mechanic of my mod). So you should use player.eat() method instead:

```
public ItemStack eat(Level pLevel, ItemStack pFood) {
      this.getFoodData().eat(pFood.getItem(), pFood, this);
      this.awardStat(Stats.ITEM_USED.get(pFood.getItem()));
      pLevel.playSound((Player)null, this.getX(), this.getY(), this.getZ(), SoundEvents.PLAYER_BURP, SoundSource.PLAYERS, 0.5F, pLevel.random.nextFloat() * 0.1F + 0.9F);
      if (this instanceof ServerPlayer) {
         CriteriaTriggers.CONSUME_ITEM.trigger((ServerPlayer)this, pFood);
      }

      return super.eat(pLevel, pFood);
   }
```

### Minecraft version

1.21.1 (and potentially every other versions)

### Farmer's Delight version

1.2.11a (and potentially every other versions)

### NeoForge version

21.1.228 (and potentially every other versions)

### Description

I’ve been noticing a bug for a couple of years now (in version 1.21.1 and potentially every other version) where you can infinitely farm flint. By using a shovel on gravel placed on a cutting board, the gravel block is never consumed, allowing for infinite flint production.

I usually fix this bug locally for myself, but I decided to report it to you. To fix it, the recipe file data/farmersdelight/recipe/cutting/gravel.json needs to be modified.

Original file:
{
  "type": "farmersdelight:cutting",
  "ingredients": [
    {
      "item": "minecraft:gravel"
    }
  ],
  "result": [
    {
      "chance": 0.9,
      "item": {
        "count": 1,
        "id": "minecraft:gravel"
      }
    },
    {
      "chance": 0.1,
      "item": {
        "count": 1,
        "id": "minecraft:flint"
      }
    }
  ],
  "tool": {
    "type": "farmersdelight:item_ability",
    "action": "shovel_dig"
  }
}



My suggested fix (I added "chance": 0.9 to the gravel output so that it isn't a permanent source of flint.): 
{
  "type": "farmersdelight:cutting",
  "ingredients": [
    {
      "item": "minecraft:gravel"
    }
  ],
  "result": [
    {
      "item": {
        "count": 1,
        "id": "minecraft:gravel"
      }
    },
    {
      "chance": 0.1,
      "item": {
        "count": 1,
        "id": "minecraft:flint"
      }
    }
  ],
  "tool": {
    "type": "farmersdelight:item_ability",
    "action": "shovel_dig"
  }
}

I understand that with this change, a player might occasionally lose a gravel block without getting any flint, but the chance is low. In my opinion, this is much better than having a flint dupe exploit.

